  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Severe weather and flash flooding are possible \nthrough tonight.\n\nVery active weather is expected over the next 12 to 24 hours, \nwith the threat for flash flooding, damaging wind gusts, and a \nfew tornadoes.\n\nAs of 06Z/2AM EDT, thunderstorms containing very heavy rain had\nprompted several Flash Flood Warnings and water rescues in\nsoutheastern Ohio. These thunderstorms are on track to reach\nwestern Maryland (Garrett County) by daybreak/6AM EDT.\n\nWill be closely evaluating the downstream environment into\nAllegany County MD, as well as Mineral, Grant, Hampshire and\nHardy Counties in WV to see if the watch needs to be expanded to\ncover this morning round of storms that have a history of\nproducing about 3 inches of rain in an hour.\n\nThe first shortwave of note which is the impetus for the\napproaching complex has an impressive swirl on radar/satellite.\nGiven the orientation of the heaviest thunderstorms in this\ncomplex, it appears that WNW to ESE training will continue as\nthis complex enters western MD. The signal for heavy rain\nbecomes a bit more diffuse as these storms cross the mountains\nthrough mid to late morning, though a persistent convergence\naxis is noted in obs/guidance trends in the above mentioned\nareas (Allegany down to Hampshire/Hardy Counties).\n\nTo the east, strong moisture advection in the low levels should\ncause at least scattered showers to break out by around or just\nbefore day break across northern VA into central MD. Instability\nis not as high here, so thunder and heavy rain may old off until\nmid to late morning. Coverage of heavier storms looks a bit \nlower and of less confidence this morning, though any heavier \nrain could still bring about a conditional flood threat in \nurban/poor drainage areas.\n\nGuidance shows a relative lull in between the wave moving in\nthis morning and another stout shortwave (currently over IL)\nmoving through late this afternoon and evening. Upstream trends\nbetween these waves in the Indianapolis area, however, show\nconvection initiating even in the middle of the night. This\nseems indicative of sufficient WAA to result in rapidly \nredeveloping convection at least in an isolated sense in between\nthese two waves at pretty much any point today.\n\nTrends will need to be very closely monitored given the very\nhigh PW airmass and potential for repeat/training thunderstorms.\nShould this heavier activity train over any one area, 3 to 6 \ninches of rain would be possible on a localized basis. This \ncould cause scattered to numerous instances of flash flooding, \nwith considerable flash flooding possible in urban/poor drainage\nareas or areas that have seen heavy rain recently. If and when \nit becomes clearer where the greatest coverage of heavy rain \nwill set up, additional Flood Watches may be issued.\n\nRegarding the severe weather threat, strong low-level winds\nwill boost SRH in a high moisture content environment. Wet\ndownbursts and brief spin ups would be possible in this\nenvironment, with the tornado threat maximized along the slowly\nlifting warm frontal zone - especially if any cell or cluster\ninteracts with/tracks along it. Shear is favorable for right-\nmoving (i.e. NW to SE) supercells if enough instability is\nrealized, and in those cases storms would be more apt to ingest\nstreamwise vorticity along the warm front (or residual outflow).\n\nThe wave this evening, though timed a bit after peak heating,\nmay pose the relative highest risk of damaging wind gusts due \nto increased low-level flow orthogonal to what will probably a \nlinear type feature.\n\nConvection (and corresponding severe/flood threats) may wane\nonly gradually tonight with continued low-level moist advection\nbeneath height falls aloft. This causes instability to remain \nor even increase through the night.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Thunderstorm chances return Wednesday night into\nThursday.\n\nAs a cold front slowly moves across our area late Wednesday and \nThursday, shower and thunderstorm chances return across most of\nour forecast region. Friday and Saturday, models continue to \ndepict a stalled front lying in our south, bringing sustained \nchances for summer-time showers and thunderstorms. Currently, \nit's unclear if the position of synoptic features and \ninstability will be sufficient for hazardous weather. For now, \nthis will be a feature to monitor as the synoptic setup becomes \nmore defined. Temperatures may fluctuate a little depending on \nwhat side of the front we're on, but likely fall some by next \nweekend, assuming the front pushes to the southeast.",
